<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/intermediate/news-vs-opinion-vs-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The news/opinion/analysis distinction is journalism\'s <strong>grammar of trust</strong>: each
    genre has a different relationship to evidence and to the reader — and keeping them legible
    is both the craft\'s discipline and the audience\'s defence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the verifiable, the explained, the argued, the label contract" widget="equationStep" config={genresCfg} />

  <h2>Reading like an editor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The tells of each genre:</strong> news leads with events and quotes; analysis with interpretation and stakes; opinion with a claim and voice. If an "analysis" piece breaks news without sourcing, or a "news" piece editorialises in adjectives — the label is lying (the critique lesson\'s reading discipline, editorial edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Sponsored content is the fourth genre:</strong> advertising wearing the newsroom\'s typography — the design lesson\'s mimicry problem; honest outlets label it loudly, and readers who know the tell (the labelling principle) can\'t be fooled by the layout.</li>
    <li><strong>Satire is a genre with its own contract:</strong> The Onion and its heirs — fiction labelled by convention; when satire travels without context (social feeds strip genre), the misinformation lesson\'s verification becomes the reader\'s skill too.</li>
    <li><strong>Why the blur is profitable:</strong> opinion drives engagement metrics; the business lesson\'s incentives explain the drift — and why the best outlets pay to keep the wall: trust is the newsroom\'s only durable asset (the finance lesson\'s compounding, reputational).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the verification lesson; SPJ Code of Ethics; the genre-labelling policies of major outlets (Reuters, AP, NYT).</p>
</TopicPage>
